<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#jebtsundamba-khutughtu">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jebtsundamba Khutughtu</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jebtsundamba Khutuktu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jebtsundamba Khutughtu is the title of the highest reincarnate lama of Mongolian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. The line began with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanabazar">Zanabazar</a> (1635–1723), son of the Tüsheet Khan, and from the third holder the incarnations were found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. The eighth, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogd-khan">Bogd Khan</a>, became ruler of independent Mongolia in 1911; after his death in 1924 the communist government forbade a successor, though later incarnations were recognised in exile.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Jebtsundamba Khutuktu is the spiritual head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> lineage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and also holds the title of Bogd Gegeen, which makes the holder the senior lama of the country. The first, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanabazar">Zanabazar</a> (1635-1723), was identified as the reincarnation of the scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taranatha">Taranatha</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jonang">Jonang</a> school. He was the son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gombodorj">Tüsheet Khan Gombodorj</a>, ruler of central Khalkha Mongolia, and became the spiritual head of the Khalkha Mongols. On 29 May 1691 he paid homage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dolon-nor">Dolon Nor</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The second Jebtsundamba Khutughtu was likewise a member of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>'s highest nobility and a direct descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>. After Chingünjav's rebellion and the second holder's death, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> decreed in 1758 that all future reincarnations were to be found among the population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/outer-mongolia">Outer Mongolia</a> declared independence from the fallen Qing dynasty in 1911, the eighth Jebtsundamba (1869-1924) was raised to theocratic ruler as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogd-khan">Bogd Khan</a> and founded the Bogd Khanate of Mongolia. He was head of state until his death in 1924.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The communist government of the Mongolian People's Republic that replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theocracy">theocracy</a> in 1924 declared that there would be no further reincarnations. A reincarnation was nevertheless found almost at once in north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, and high lamas of the previous holder's retinue went to interview the child's mother, Tsendjav. In July 1925 the Central Committee of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolian-peoples-party">Mongolian People's Revolutionary Party</a> decided to turn the matter over to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thubten-gyatso">13th Dalai Lama</a>, whose decision would be subject to new legislation separating church and state. In February 1929 the installation of any new Khutukhtu was forbidden. A ninth Jebtsundamba Khutukhtu, born Jampal Namdol Chökyi Gyaltsen in 1932, was installed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/14th-dalai-lama">14th Dalai Lama</a> as head of a reinvigorated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jonang">Jonang</a> lineage, and he died in early 2012.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jebtsundamba Khutuktu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Zanabazar, son of Tüsheet Khan Gombodorj, became the first <b>Jebtsundamba Khutughtu</b>, the head of Mongolian Buddhism, as well as a famous sculptor.</blockquote><a href="/state/tusheet-khanate">Tüsheet Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
